34824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534953 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534953 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534953 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534953 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da2b25ef-5cc5-53ca-8aad-45786fb6f09c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534959 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8b2b3c4-71d4-527c-abf3-f3422fc41129" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534972 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534972 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534972 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534972 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7e/7eb8812e90e4862a3658773186fe3809b212d58d5d22613fac6d9067ae4ccbc9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534972 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "059b810f-7faa-5870-a600-8d8d6445cd02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534972 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,⟹\\,x={e}^{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f0/f0ddb5b21bdff9cbed426abed5bf9b94c4c4c4d742e2eb90dcdb92895c2adf3a.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(e-{e}^{y})^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2y}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534973 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534973 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534973 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/54/5453dfc44e11cad377c1e8e2c30fc23d537d4423d38fab4428395362f51d9412.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={y}^{2}+1,$ the vertical line $x=5,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=5.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08dd2009-a210-5f29-bbaa-81488a5664f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534973 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=5$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+1 & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/e8/e83c5729ef5db920a0b451a8571ad84f45d2b4e950ab831ced0da2c0a12cea50.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={y}^{2}+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,5-({y}^{2}+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(16-8{y}^{2}+{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(16y-\\frac{8}{3}{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{5})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (32-\\frac{64}{3}+\\frac{32}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{256\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534977 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534977 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534977 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534977 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the region bounded by the curve $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ the vertical line $x=3,$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534977 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ccddc8e-6343-5b87-aa99-4a041586cf69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534977 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/47/47b612a3e08ff24055cbffa7d53fb337f1905ec861abd3a8e535088d383162cb.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/19/1927874f5eca0c18b7b61b37d0e6fac5198ec9dcf89205763e88570287330292.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(\\sqrt[3]{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-\\sqrt[3]{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}-6{y}^{1/3}+9)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}y-6{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}y+9{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{3{y}^{5/3}}{5}{|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{9{y}^{4/3}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+9y{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{3}{5}-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+(9-0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{51\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50022" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534986 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/10/1066e10a5a9860e371ca2895ec39f7cdce4e000d5cca9f3b478f57cd890d647a.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ for $x\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],$ and the horizontal line $y=\\frac{1}{2}$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "236b0649-a813-5e30-b589-a9199c88af15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534986 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4c086c9a3a99609728bc7c53f5a28ac86431e5c37e2b87bcfd3031a32aa470.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=\\frac{1}{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{[\\,(\\frac{1}{x})-(\\frac{1}{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}{(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534988 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/14/144259aa150936de0e3425c13f953da0833d87c2aba1304aa39b3c47fda03550.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1,$ the horizontal line $y=1,$  the horizontal line $y=0,$ and the vertical line $x=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1$, as shown above. Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2588c644-3085-550f-abbf-182631d5c4e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534988 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left function and which is the right, let's draw our horizontal arrow.\n\n![](images/8a/8a17502c300fae56e9f2e1706d23a3e5a7d3233eebff9a77958d117a0135adb0.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=6\\sqrt{y}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the resulting solid is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(6\\sqrt{y}+1)-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{y}+2)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24\\sqrt{y}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(36y+24{y}^{1/2}+4)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(36\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+24\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3/2}+4y)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(18(1)^{2}+16(1)^{3/2}+4(1))-0] \\\\  & =38\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534989 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/68/68359143b50b4d6187f2d50793b1d3cb4b48656f1996cd222cb5d07f666b535e.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=-1-x,$ the vertical line $x=-3,$ and the horizontal line $y=0$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-3,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27279421-a932-5375-bd1a-4824ccac1d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534989 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=-1-x\\,⟹\\,x=-1-y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=-3$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1-y & =-3 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/73/73d5da7e1bd4b409574f6e9ab034dde51012eb956b3ea72a3529f7d432fe781d.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=-1-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-1-y)-(-3)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-y)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534992 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9d8f2d52a0d943a4eeb1d323cc90db145c9b87b57e87d595a7ca5809498e7a6a.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x=y+1,$ the vertical line $x=4,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=4.$\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f10ec35-272f-563f-8af5-2c0fc2e74c5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534992 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersection of the curve and the line $x=4$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =4 \\\\ y & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=3$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/f9/f9fd1773b9599b1a4b61fc58c854bf998ed147a9707f719a80a3588874c0eb4f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=y+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[4-(y+1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(3-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(3-y)^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-{3}^{3}) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534995 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8f/8fa5e5b2300c3395d48f5355352cd6ece7477f3bae8e8c185f0f869fa197b0e9.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "31757f7a-27f2-5cad-90a3-e9168497ad54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534995 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2)+(x-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/5a/5a1821ff500c901d9f072fe56101f17e579018d0710543c4a2e371214894725c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(2)-({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(2-x+2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535001 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535001 #edb/ref 1069 "q-352955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535001 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535001 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0e/0e402d36061211a9e51df852f7fa595eb212e5c6ce9b3d43a2d53f9bea1fedd3.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=(x-1)^{4}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=1.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535001 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fd041ce-cfe7-560a-8362-3b96b6047662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535001 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{4} & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/2a/2a2e4be26d18e408bf6e4580c72ec2f3c08eed1750d38e0716f06a32d3c03e7c.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=(x-1)^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,1-(x-1)^{4}\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-2(x-1)^{4}+(x-1)^{8})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(x-\\frac{2}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(x-1)^{9}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([2-\\frac{2}{5}(1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(1)^{9}\\text{ }]-[0-\\frac{2}{5}(-1)^{5}+\\frac{1}{9}(-1)^{9}\\text{ }]) \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi }{45}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535002 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535002 #edb/ref 1069 "q-353047" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535002 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535002 #edb/ref 1071 "The region enclosed by the curve $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=5$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=5.$ Find the volume $V$ of the resulting solid. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535002 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4017e457-d6e9-5799-a313-b509dcbd530f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535002 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/ad/ad089d39428498123469a2ac7895f7d04b8b3ff9dac18a1698462bfc7fabae99.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/97/975f68d06bb52e162ad792987899509f4090c03ec1b55af6335982a2951f6bbf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=4-(x-1)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=5.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,5-(4-(x-1)^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{(1+(x-1)^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1+2(x-1)^{2}+(x-1)^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}((x-1)^{4}+2(x-1)^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}(x-1)^{5}+\\frac{2}{3}(x-1)^{3}+x)\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{2}{3}+2-(-\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{2}{3}+0)) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{43}{15}+\\frac{13}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{56\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535006 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535006 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535006 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535006 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9d0b7f238afe0f6cb82bb8670b9589aa10490b6e776e7f4a991a561aa0ad2245.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{4-x}$ and the horizontal line $y=1$ from $x=0$ to $x=3,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$about the line $y=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535006 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48038fbf-e0e6-55ac-98d1-e8c48a8b376a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535006 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=3$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/73/73fc1e2cad0969a629e2c2c4a55ce8ac66c16b67fe2984e8b75dc6aa3a5eeace.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=\\sqrt{4-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,(\\sqrt{4-x})-(1)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}((4-x)-2\\sqrt{4-x}+1)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(5-x-2\\sqrt{4-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (5{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x+2{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{4-x}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\pi (5x{|}_{0}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{3}+\\frac{4}{3}(4-x)^{3/2}{|}_{0}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi ((15-0)-(\\frac{9}{2}-0)+\\frac{4}{3}(1-8)) \\\\  & =\\pi (15-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535008 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535008 #edb/ref 1069 "q-120816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535008 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535008 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5d/5d154a914d58f5b695adec9dd873ac067ba8280a4fefffd4f2f5559ff5ee04da.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{1}{1-x},$ the vertical line $x=2,$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535008 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4fe06b9c-2dd8-5751-a283-a97db5419aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535008 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1-x}\\,⟹\\,x=1-\\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ to $y=-1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/23/239113c4d2bbca26c694796f6f17bd26bebf47697dad53f7af832088213fd4c9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=1-\\frac{1}{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{[\\,(2)-(1-\\frac{1}{y})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{(1+\\frac{1}{y})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{-1}(1+\\frac{2}{y}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50007" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535017 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/848b73762d79b0b7b898f3c011e4118256b5fca37fa9d702313bc16da04b4c3d.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [0,1],$ and the horizontal line $y=2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=2.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f36b991-1cba-5c10-9333-13a2ade76e0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535017 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/3b/3b634be23e06f6038e00b91f27eadacd1d1bcc51516d741786cc433a1ce731ce.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=2{x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(2)-(2{x}^{2})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4{x}^{4}-8{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535020 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534887 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85996" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535020 #edb/ref 1071 "The region enclosed by the curve $y={e}^{x},$ the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=1,$ and the horizontal line $y=3$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b9b18aa-2c78-5504-8e8f-08d14250da87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535020 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/72/72b701d2404e1acdcfba6b1994588419afc8246de6df8bf50e93baca0051df6a.png)\n\nNotice that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1$ along the $x$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/89/89d6b6d2d65f161353da5fea9175aaf780f0fdc38405b855fbbc033349a484db.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={e}^{x},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({e}^{x})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(3-{e}^{x})}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(9-6{e}^{x}+{e}^{2x})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(9x-6{e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ([9-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\text{ }]-[-6+\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{29}{2}-6e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(29-12e+{e}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81786" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535024 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/46/4643824b623d5325f53407147f086a95073db91be0148381780668c420ec7c45.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by the curve $x={e}^{y},$ the vertical line $x=e,$ and the horizontal line $y=0,$ as shown in the picture. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=e.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6ffaec2e-3741-5b7e-88f5-bdff517c9f5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535024 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=e$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =e \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/93/93fd0875ea2ba1d1d92bb781a61cfb114ac88b7d240cd825b4842f686f7c760f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x={e}^{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=e.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(e)-({e}^{y})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[e-{e}^{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{2}-2{e}^{y+1}+{e}^{2y})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y+1}\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2y}\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\pi ({e}^{2}y\\,{|}_{0}^{1}-2{e}^{y+1}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{{e}^{2y}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =\\pi (({e}^{2}-0)-2({e}^{2}-e)+(\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\pi (-{e}^{2}+2e+\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(4e-{e}^{2}-1)\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(-1+4e-{e}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535050 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535050 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535050 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535050 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d2/d27538da9cf9ebb39324477a9c3e9eabd950b648507ebc616a11d5c8a6054a19.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ for $x\\in [0,2],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535050 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a85d5623-8f4e-5539-b87a-051d9e911395" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535050 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/94/94d3f7e76db23c387366024bdd5dbb25f8adddc25d78bb3fc81f5900b4128fcf.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y={x}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,({x}^{3})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{6}+2{x}^{3}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535051 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535051 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535051 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535051 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/42/421d588cd290294cf6471704f2459c22b00c8b47756b808e9da267a8007792b2.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=2$ is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the line $x=2,$ as shown in the picture. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535051 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a978de85-c2b4-579a-92f4-5064fe99c547" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535051 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe take the positive sign since $x\\ge 0.$\n\nNow, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line $x=2$ by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}} & =2 \\\\ y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=8$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/c7/c762c0e41cf8862535e5241a00149fdc6e409c3f79630a411cff73fcd11759b0.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{[\\,(2)-(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}{(\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}-2)}^{2}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{8}(\\frac{y}{2}-4\\sqrt{\\frac{y}{2}}+4)\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535058 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/29/293593a043a7713d87a2f8ad8a2eaecee26b34288e4662bb4b2fce834e703e56.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3},$ the vertical line $x=-1$ and the horizontal lines $y=0$ and $y=1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following integrals gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b4260f61-3124-52b4-9558-94f62f160010" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535058 #edb/ref 1074 "Since our region rotates about a vertical line, we need to write the equation of the given curve in terms of $y:$ \n\n$$\ny={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, notice that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1$ along the $y$-axis.\n\nTo determine which is the left and which is the right function, let's draw our horizontal arrow.  \n\n![](images/60/600ef6d7265add5c669a29394e45facae040292ce19caad9dcae390979b604b9.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right function})-(\\text{left function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\sqrt[3]{y})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\sqrt[3]{y}+1)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2/3}+2{y}^{1/3}+1)\\,\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-49890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535077 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/28/2817662752008c41b76d1beb9bec73907f55d836524fe48c583997670421f70b.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y=2{x}^{2},$ for $x\\in [-2,\\frac{5}{2}],$ and the horizontal line $y=-1$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the resulting solid?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e80b3506-231f-529b-b14d-6bb0a866165f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535077 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that we are given that the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e1/e1774466f020515d3296f2e48fda0a61154bddbfd2285a67c97bdc7e35b60da5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y=-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=2{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}[\\,(2{x}^{2})-(-1)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(2{x}^{2}+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{5/2}(4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535082 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535082 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535082 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535082 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c2/c21d4794aeedcc70fe037a936724d2708a484d43c457b2d120927f86f1fa6ffc.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x-2$ and the horizontal line $y=-2$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $y=-2.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535082 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f0334985-4e7c-56a4-ba8b-035c69ca7733" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535082 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we want to determine the limits of integration. We find the intersections of the curve and the line by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-2 & =-2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2$ along the $x$-axis. \n\nTo determine which is the lower function and which is the upper, let's draw our vertical arrow.\n\n![](images/e7/e785138362c91f9034d90ee9d923fc818cd299b3ee5d6d25eb1bf40cabc2f2b0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the lower function $y={x}^{2}-2x-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the upper function $y=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}[\\,(\\text{upper function})-(\\text{lower function})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\,(-2)-({x}^{2}-2x-2)\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(-{x}^{2}+2x)^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}-{x}^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3}\\text{ })|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi ([\\frac{1}{5}(2)^{5}-(2)^{4}+\\frac{4}{3}(2)^{3}\\text{ }]-[0]) \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{32}{5}-16+\\frac{32}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
